      "title": "Wooden Handicrafts of Iran: Latticework and Inlay | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iranian geometric woodwork, architectural lattices, and khatam inlay, with attention to technique and historical evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-woodwork",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the construction before the pattern",
        "content": "A patterned wooden object can belong to quite different craft traditions. In Iranian architectural woodwork, gereh-sazi describes both open lattice construction and assembled geometric panels. The first creates a framework that may remain open or receive glass or other inserts; the second fits shapes together into a continuous decorative surface. A repeated star alone therefore does not identify the technique. Look at whether the design makes a screen, forms a panel, or decorates another object. [s1]\n\nThese distinctions connect household furnishings with architecture. Wooden grilles and panels belonged to private houses and palaces as well as religious buildings. Woodcraft should consequently be understood through the spaces and uses it served, rather than treated only as portable ornament. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "lattice-and-survival",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Lattices, light, and surviving evidence",
        "content": "Safavid examples survive in Isfahan’s Hasht Behesht and Chehel Sotun palaces. Window lattices could incorporate clear or colored glass, creating a boundary through which light still passed. Related geometric compositions also decorated ceilings. During the Qajar period, floral and curving patterns became more prominent, and mirrored glass enlarged the decorative vocabulary. [s1]\n\nSurvival is uneven. Insects, fire, and the passage of time have destroyed wooden objects, so the earliest surviving example cannot automatically establish when a technique began. Sheila Blair’s survey documents medieval examples while emphasizing this loss of evidence. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "inlay-and-craft-centers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Inlay and the history of craft centers",
        "content": "Khatam is another important category: decorative inlay associated particularly with Shiraz and Isfahan. Habib Borjian’s account identifies it as a specialty of these cities from the Zand period in the later eighteenth century. Its history belongs within a wider craft economy that included small workshops, domestic production, and rural industries. [s2]\n\nThat economy repeatedly changed. Borjian cautions against assuming an uninterrupted passage of skills from Safavid Isfahan into later workshops. War, population loss, imported manufactured goods, and shifting demand disrupted production. Twentieth-century craft education, restoration, and tourism subsequently helped reshape the market. [s2]\n\nAn object is therefore most informative when its technique, maker, place, and date are documented together. A city’s reputation supplies useful context, but cannot establish the origin or age of an individual piece. This distinction allows appreciation of both inherited skills and later adaptation without turning every patterned box or window into evidence of an unchanged ancient tradition. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
